{
 "id": "2016-veravillarroel-perception",
 "name": "Perception of Bike Lane Colour: Safety and Liking (15 colour-design pictures)",
 "description": "Rebuilt from Vera-Villarroel et al. (2016), \"Perception of Safety and Liking Associated to the Colour Intervention of Bike Lanes: Contribution from the Behavioural Sciences to Urban Design and Wellbeing\", PLOS ONE 11(8): e0160399, with the authors' own pictures (Figs 1–3, CC BY 4.0).\n\nThe authors photographed three real bike-lane intersections in Santiago, Chile (fully painted, line-delimited, and partially painted in a chessboard pattern) and digitally recoloured the pavement treatment in five colours: yellow, blue, green, white and red. All 15 colour-design pictures are shown one at a time in random order. For each, participants rate three statements on a 5-point agreement scale (1 strongly disagree, 5 strongly agree): the colour is clearly distinguishable (saliency), the colour seems pleasant (liking), and the colour seems appropriate for my safety (perceived safety). Records are keyed design_colour (e.g. chess_red). The paper (N = 538 Chilean adults) used repeated-measures comparisons and MANCOVA of colour × design; red scored highest, then yellow and blue, and colour mattered far more than design.\n\nDifferences from the original study: (1) the pictures are cut from the published figure panels (about 600 × 420 px) rather than the original files shown at 20.5 × 11.5 cm; (2) the answer buttons label every scale point (the paper's figure shows only the agreement anchors); (3) only gender and age are asked; (4) the original survey was part of a larger wellbeing questionnaire, which is not included.",
 "author": "Vera-Villarroel et al.",
